STEP 1 -- baseline on the wire: with no endpoint, the DUT sends NO MED TLV     PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: LLDP-MED only appears under certain conditions.  Before creating those
  conditions, show that the thing being tested is really absent.  Then its later
  appearance is a measurement and not a pre-existing state.
  Capture on the box: tools/tb capture tb277 lldp1159 eth3 40 m1_base.pcap ether proto 0x88cc

tb277$ tools/tb tool tb277 lldp1159 lldpwire /tmp/lldp1159/m1_base.pcap --src e2:1a:ea:d4:70:43 --tlv
    pcap      /tmp/lldp1159/m1_base.pcap
    source e2:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 01:80:c2:00:00:0e
      --- frame at 1792550955.713, 4 TLVs ---
        Chassis ID                         MAC address: e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43
        Port ID                            Interface name: port1.0.10
        Time To Live                       120 s
        OUI e01aea subtype 2               0c26573a87c541829d436cb04a0e77c2

  <-- Four TLVs: the three mandatory ones plus one Allied Telesis organisationally
      specific TLV.  There is no LLDP-MED TLV of any kind.
  <-- `show lldp interface` reports all four MED TLVs selected (`McNpLoPe--`), and the
      wire carries none of them.  Selection is not advertisement.
  => STEP 1 PASS: the baseline is established.  With no voice policy and no LLDP-MED
     endpoint on the port, the DUT sends a plain 4-TLV advertisement and no MED TLV.

STEP 2 -- a voice VLAN creates a Network Policy, but does NOT put it on the wire  PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: this is the step that tells "configured" from "advertised".  The show command
  will report that the policy exists.  The question is whether the wire carries it.

awplus(config)#vlan database
awplus(config-vlan)#vlan 70 name ckVOICE
awplus(config-vlan)#vlan 70 state enable
awplus(config)#interface port1.0.10
awplus(config-if)#switchport voice vlan 70
awplus(config-if)#switchport voice vlan priority 5
awplus(config-if)#switchport voice dscp 46

  No `% ` line on any of them.

awplus#show vlan brief
    70      ckVOICE          STATIC  ACTIVE  port1.0.10(t)
awplus#show running-config interface port1.0.10
    interface port1.0.10
     switchport
     switchport mode access
     switchport access vlan 10
     spanning-tree portfast
     switchport voice vlan 70
     switchport voice dscp 46
awplus#show lldp local-info med interface port1.0.10
      Network Policy ................... Voice
           VLAN ID ..................... 70
           Tagged Flag ................. Tagged
           Layer-2 Priority ............ 5
           DSCP Value .................. 46

  <-- The voice VLAN joins the port TAGGED, shown as `port1.0.10(t)`, while the access
      VLAN stays untagged.  The inherited underlay was not disturbed, and that was
      checked and not assumed: a ping from tb277 eth3 to the DUT's 10.38.22.34 returned
      3 received, 0% packet loss with the voice VLAN in place.
  <-- `switchport voice vlan` silently added `spanning-tree portfast` to the interface.
      Nobody typed that line and it was not there before.  It is a teardown hazard: a
      tester who removes only what they typed leaves it behind.
  <-- `switchport voice vlan priority 5` was accepted but is NOT in the running-config,
      while `switchport voice dscp 46` is.  5 is the default voice priority, and only a
      value that is not the default is stored.  STEP 6 confirms this from the other side.

  Measurement, with the policy fully configured and no MED endpoint on the port:

tb277$ tools/tb tool tb277 lldp1159 lldpwire /tmp/lldp1159/m3_novoiceend.pcap --src e2:1a:ea:d4:70:43 --tlv
    pcap      /tmp/lldp1159/m3_novoiceend.pcap        (a 40 s capture)
      --- frame at 1792551045.796, 4 TLVs ---
        Chassis ID / Port ID / Time To Live / OUI e01aea subtype 2

  <-- Still four TLVs.  The Network Policy the DUT says it has is not on the wire.
  => STEP 2 PASS: the DUT built the Network Policy from the voice VLAN configuration, and
     reports it correctly.  It does not advertise it, because no LLDP-MED endpoint has
     been seen on that port.  That is the behaviour TIA-1057 asks for.  A verdict taken
     from `show lldp local-info med` alone would have reported a policy that no frame
     carried.

STEP 3 -- a real LLDP-MED endpoint makes the DUT advertise the policy          PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: present a real LLDP-MED Endpoint Class III IP phone on the port, then read the
  DUT's reply out of the capture and compare it field by field with the configuration.

tb277$ tools/tb sudotool tb277 lldp1159 lldpmed_phone eth3 /tmp/lldp1159/m4_phone.pcap \
         --lldp-secs 14 --no-dhcp
    15:51:16 phase 1: LLDP-MED from 00:00:5e:00:53:10 on eth3 for 14s
      sent 14 LLDPDUs
      voice network policy: {'app': 1, 'unknown': 0, 'tagged': 1, 'vlan': 70, 'l2prio': 5, 'dscp': 46}
    no DHCP phase (vlan=70)
    exit 0

tb277$ tools/tb tool tb277 lldp1159 lldpwire /tmp/lldp1159/m4_phone.pcap --src e2:1a:ea:d4:70:43 --tlv
    pcap      /tmp/lldp1159/m4_phone.pcap
    packets   57 total, 3 LLDP from e2:1a:ea:d4:70:43

    source e2:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 01:80:c2:00:00:0e
      frames 3 over 2.0 s
      inter-frame gap  min 1.00 s  mean 1.00 s  max 1.00 s
      --- frame at 1792551077.827, 7 TLVs ---
        Chassis ID                         MAC address: e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43
        Port ID                            Interface name: port1.0.10
        Time To Live                       120 s
        OUI e01aea subtype 2               0c26573a87c541829d436cb04a0e77c2
        802.3 MAC/PHY Configuration/Status 038001001e
        MED LLDP-MED Capabilities          caps=[Capabilities, Network Policy, Location ID, Inventory] device type=Network Connectivity
        MED Network Policy                 application=Voice vlan=70 tagged=True priority=5 dscp=46 unknown_policy=False

  (all three frames are identical; the other two are at 1792551078.829 and 1792551079.830)

  The advertisement went from 4 TLVs to 7, and every new field matches the configuration:

    configured                          advertised in the frame                   match
    switchport voice vlan 70            Network Policy vlan=70, tagged=True       yes
    switchport voice vlan priority 5    Network Policy priority=5                 yes
    switchport voice dscp 46            Network Policy dscp=46                    yes
    (the application type)              application=Voice (TIA application type 1) yes
    the DUT is a switch                 MED Capabilities device type=Network Connectivity  yes
    the capability list in show         caps=[Capabilities, Network Policy,       yes
    lldp local-info med                 Location ID, Inventory]

  <-- The 802.3 MAC/PHY Configuration/Status TLV also appears, although no
      `lldp tlv-select mac-phy-config` was configured.  LLDP-MED requires it of a
      Network Connectivity device, so the DUT adds it by itself when it enters MED mode
      on a port.  An 802.3 TLV can therefore appear on a port whose 802.3 selection
      column reads `--------`.
  <-- Two independent decoders agree.  tools/lldpmed_phone.py parsed the Network Policy
      as {'app': 1, 'tagged': 1, 'vlan': 70, 'l2prio': 5, 'dscp': 46}.  tools/lldpwire.py,
      which was written separately, parsed the same bytes as `application=Voice vlan=70
      tagged=True priority=5 dscp=46`.  The 12-bit VLAN, 3-bit priority and 6-bit DSCP
      packing is the easiest part of LLDP-MED to decode wrongly, and two tools built
      independently from the standard read it the same way.
  => STEP 3 PASS: when a real LLDP-MED endpoint appeared on the port, the DUT advertised
     the Network Policy TLV, and the TLV carried exactly the VLAN, tagging, priority and
     DSCP that were configured.

STEP 4 -- the DUT classifies the endpoint as Class III, with a non-MED control PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: the other half of STEP 3.  The DUT must also recognise what the endpoint is.
  The control is a plain, non-MED neighbour on the SAME port at the SAME time.  Without
  it, a correct classification could just be the port's default.

awplus#show lldp neighbors
    Total number of neighbors on these ports .... 5

    Local    Neighbor        Neighbor        Neighbor                System   MED
    Port     Chassis ID      Port ID         Sys Name                Cap.     Ty Pwr
    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    1.0.2    0000.cd42.3029  port1.0.2
    1.0.4    889d.98df.2602  port1.0.4
    1.0.10   0000.5e00.5310  0000.5e00.5310  ck-phone                -----T-- 3
    1.0.14   889d.98df.2602  port1.0.14
    1.0.16   0000.cd42.3029  port1.0.16

  <-- `-----T--` is Telephone in the capability codes this command prints.  `MED Ty = 3`
      is Endpoint Class III.
  <-- The four other rows are the passive partner switches on other ports.  They are not
      part of this case's measurement.

awplus#show lldp neighbors detail interface port1.0.10
      Chassis ID Type .................. MAC address
      Chassis ID ....................... 0000.5e00.5310
      Port ID Type ..................... MAC address
      System Name ...................... ck-phone
      System Capabilities - Supported .. Telephone
                          - Enabled .... Telephone
      LLDP-MED Device Type ............. Endpoint Class III
      LLDP-MED Capabilities ............ LLDP-MED Capabilities, Network Policy,
                                         Extended Power - PD, Inventory
      Inventory Management:
           Hardware Revision ........... CK-PHONE-EMU

  <-- Every field is what the endpoint advertises, including the inventory string
      CK-PHONE-EMU.
  <-- The DUT reads the endpoint's power capability as `Extended Power - PD`, a powered
      device, where its own is `[not supported]`.  The two sides are read separately and
      correctly.

  The control.  A plain LLDPDU was sent with tools/lldpsend.py, advertising Station-only
  capability and no MED TLV at all, while the phone entry was still live:

awplus#show lldp neighbors interface port1.0.10
    Total number of neighbors on these ports .... 2
    1.0.10   0000.5e00.5310  0000.5e00.5310  ck-phone                -----T-- 3
    1.0.10   0200.0000.00f0  ckPLAIN         ckplain                 -------S

  <-- One port, one instant, two neighbours, two classifications.  The MED phone reads
      `-----T--` with `MED Ty 3`.  The plain neighbour reads `-------S`, Station only,
      and its MED columns are blank.
  => STEP 4 PASS: the DUT classified the LLDP-MED endpoint as Endpoint Class III and
     Telephone, and read its inventory and power capability correctly.  A non-MED
     neighbour on the same port at the same time got no MED classification.  So the DUT
     classifies by what the frame advertises, not by the port the frame arrived on.

STEP 5 -- LLDP-MED Fast Start: three frames, one second apart, and its trigger PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: `show lldp` reports `Fast Start Count ... 3`.  Fast start exists so a phone
  plugged into a port learns its voice VLAN in seconds instead of waiting out a 30 s
  timer.  It is a timing behaviour, so it can only be graded from frame timestamps.

  The measurement, from the same capture as STEP 3:
    source e2:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 01:80:c2:00:00:0e
      frames 3 over 2.0 s
      inter-frame gap  min 1.00 s  mean 1.00 s  max 1.00 s

  <-- Exactly three frames at 1.00 s, with no spread.  This DUT's steady-state rate on
      the same port is 30.03 s.  `Fast Start Count 3` is three frames, and the fast-start
      interval is one second.

  What triggers it.  The burst did not happen every time a MED frame arrived, so the
  trigger was pinned down with four observations instead of assumed:

    run            MED endpoint   was it NEW to the port?                        burst?
    m4_phone       ...53:10       yes, first appearance                          3 frames @ 1.00 s
    m8_prio6       ...53:10       yes, its TTL-120 entry had expired at ~1792551197  3 frames @ 1.00 s
    m9_nopolicy    ...53:10       no, entry still live (valid to ~1792551376)    none in a 10 s window
    m11_newmac     ...53:20       yes, a different MAC is a different endpoint   3 frames @ 1.00 s

  <-- The rule the evidence supports: the fast-start burst fires when an LLDP-MED
      endpoint becomes NEW on the port.  It does not fire on every MED frame received.
  <-- m11_newmac is the decisive one.  It used a different endpoint MAC to create a new
      neighbour on demand, instead of waiting out a TTL, and the burst fired at once.
      It also fired with the Network Policy TLV deselected, so fast start is triggered by
      the endpoint and not by having a policy to send.
  <-- One reading had to be corrected by arithmetic, and it is recorded so nobody repeats
      it.  A 40 s capture taken at 1792551212 showed a 4-TLV frame with no MED TLVs, with
      the phone's entry apparently still in the table.  The phone's last frame was at
      about 1792551077 with TTL 120, so its entry expired at about 1792551197, which is
      15 s BEFORE that frame.  There was no MED endpoint on the port at that moment, so
      correctly there was no MED TLV.  STEP 7 settles the general question with a capture
      taken while an endpoint was live.
  => STEP 5 PASS: LLDP-MED Fast Start sent exactly 3 frames at 1.00 s, which matches the
     configured `Fast Start Count 3`, against a steady state of 30.03 s.  The trigger is
     an endpoint becoming new on the port, shown by four observations including one
     driven with a deliberately different endpoint MAC.

STEP 6 -- the advertisement follows the configuration: priority 5 becomes 6    PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: STEP 3 proved one policy was advertised correctly.  This proves the
  advertisement tracks a change in the configuration.  It also checks the
  default-priority observation from STEP 2 from the other side.

awplus(config-if)#switchport voice vlan priority 6
awplus#show running-config interface port1.0.10
     switchport voice vlan 70
     switchport voice vlan priority 6
     switchport voice dscp 46
awplus#show lldp local-info med interface port1.0.10
           Layer-2 Priority ............ 6

  <-- `switchport voice vlan priority 6` IS stored, where `priority 5` never was.  This
      confirms STEP 2: the voice priority is written back only when it is not the
      default of 5.

tb277$ tools/tb tool tb277 lldp1159 lldpwire /tmp/lldp1159/m8_prio6.pcap --src e2:1a:ea:d4:70:43 --tlv
      --- frame at 1792551247.982, 7 TLVs ---
        MED Network Policy                 application=Voice vlan=70 tagged=True priority=6 dscp=46 unknown_policy=False

  <-- The VLAN and the DSCP did not move.  Exactly the field that was reconfigured is
      exactly the field that changed on the wire.
  => STEP 6 PASS: changing the voice priority from 5 to 6 changed the advertised Network
     Policy TLV from priority=5 to priority=6, and left the VLAN and DSCP alone.

STEP 7 -- two independent removal paths, each proven on the wire               PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: removal must always be proven.  LLDP-MED has two different ways to stop
  advertising a network policy: switch the TLV off, or remove the policy's content.  Both
  are tested, and both must leave the rest of LLDP-MED working.

  7a -- `no lldp med-tlv-select network-policy` (the TLV selection)

awplus(config-if)#no lldp med-tlv-select network-policy
awplus#show lldp interface port1.0.10
     1.0.10   Rx Tx  -- --  10.38.22.34      ---------- -------- -------- Mc--LoPe--

  <-- `Np` is gone from the MED column.  `Mc`, `Lo` and `Pe` remain.  The voice VLAN
      configuration is untouched: `switchport voice vlan 70`, `priority 6` and `dscp 46`
      are all still in the running-config.

tb277$ tools/tb tool tb277 lldp1159 lldpwire /tmp/lldp1159/m11_newmac.pcap --src e2:1a:ea:d4:70:43 --tlv
      frames 3 over 2.0 s        <- the fast-start burst still fires
      --- frame, 6 TLVs ---
        Chassis ID / Port ID / Time To Live / OUI e01aea subtype 2
        802.3 MAC/PHY Configuration/Status 038001001e
        MED LLDP-MED Capabilities          caps=[Capabilities, Network Policy, Location ID, Inventory] device type=Network Connectivity

  <-- Seven TLVs became six.  The Network Policy TLV is gone and MED Capabilities stays.
  <-- The endpoint-side tool agrees on its own: `voice network policy: None`.

tb277$ tools/tb tool tb277 lldp1159 lldpwire /tmp/lldp1159/m10_nopolicy_periodic.pcap --src e2:1a:ea:d4:70:43 --tlv
      frames 2 over 30.0 s        (a 70 s capture with a MED endpoint live on the port)
      inter-frame gap  min 30.03 s  mean 30.03 s  max 30.03 s
      --- frame at 1792551340.062, 6 TLVs ---
        ... MED LLDP-MED Capabilities ... (no Network Policy TLV)

  <-- Two things at once.  The removal holds in the ordinary 30 s advertisement as well
      as in the fast-start burst.  And, answering the question STEP 5 raised: while a MED
      endpoint is live on the port, the ordinary 30 s frames DO carry MED TLVs.
  <-- Ordinary LLDP is completely unaffected: still 30.03 s, still TTL 120.

  The arc closes.  `lldp med-tlv-select network-policy` restored gives `McNpLoPe--`
  again, and to a new endpoint (MAC ...53:30) the phone emulator reports:
    MED-network-policy {'app': 1, 'unknown': 0, 'tagged': 1, 'vlan': 70, 'l2prio': 6, 'dscp': 46}

  <-- The policy is back, and it still carries the priority-6 value from STEP 6.

  7b -- `no switchport voice vlan` (the policy's content)

awplus(config-if)#no switchport voice vlan
awplus#show lldp local-info med interface port1.0.10
      LLDP-MED Capabilities ............ LLDP-MED Capabilities, Network Policy, ...
      Network Policy ................... [not configured]

  <-- Back to the STEP 0 wording.

tb277$ tools/tb tool tb277 lldp1159 lldpwire /tmp/lldp1159/m14_novlan.pcap --src e2:1a:ea:d4:70:43 --tlv
      frames 3 over 2.0 s        <- the fast-start burst still fires
      --- frame at 1792551505.211, 6 TLVs ---
        ... MED LLDP-MED Capabilities ... (no Network Policy TLV)

  <-- Six TLVs again, the same result on the wire as 7a, reached by a completely
      different route.  The TLV is still selected, because `Np` is in the column, but
      there is no policy to put in it, so none is sent.  A device that advertised an
      empty or stale policy here would be at fault.  This one advertises nothing.
  <-- A teardown hazard, measured: after `no switchport voice vlan`, the lines
      `switchport voice vlan priority 6` and `switchport voice dscp 46` REMAIN in the
      running-config with no voice VLAN for them to describe.  They are not removed with
      the VLAN and must each be removed on their own.  With the `spanning-tree portfast`
      line of STEP 2, that is three lines a careless teardown leaves behind.
  => STEP 7 PASS: both removal paths work and both were proven on the wire.  Deselecting
     the TLV and removing the voice VLAN each took the advertisement from 7 TLVs to 6.
     In both cases MED Capabilities, the fast-start burst and ordinary LLDP at 30.03 s
     kept working, and both arcs were closed.

STEP 8 -- the LLDP-MED configuration survives a cold boot, measured on the wire PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: the voice VLAN was restored, leaving the priority at 6.  6 is deliberately NOT
  the default of 5, so a device that had silently gone back to defaults could never be
  mistaken for one that persisted.  The configuration was saved, the DUT was reloaded,
  and a fresh endpoint was then presented to the rebooted switch.

awplus#show lldp local-info med interface port1.0.10
      Network Policy ................... Voice
           VLAN ID ..................... 70
           Layer-2 Priority ............ 6
           DSCP Value .................. 46
awplus#write
    Building configuration...
    [OK]

  What the `write` added to startup-config was read and diffed, not assumed:
    ! Startup-config saved on Wed Oct 21 02:38:03 2026  ->  02:58:50 2026 +0000
    + vlan 70 name ckVOICE
    - vlan 10,48,60 state enable   ->  + vlan 10,48,60,70 state enable
      (under interface port1.0.10)
    + spanning-tree portfast
    + switchport voice vlan 70
    + switchport voice vlan priority 6
    + switchport voice dscp 46

  <-- `spanning-tree portfast`, the line the DUT added by itself in STEP 2, is saved with
      the rest.

awplus#reload
    reboot system? (y/n): y
    15:59:25 NZDT  reload issued
    16:00:25 NZDT  login seen after 60 s

  After the cold boot, the configuration:
awplus#show running-config interface port1.0.10
    interface port1.0.10
     switchport
     switchport mode access
     switchport access vlan 10
     spanning-tree portfast
     switchport voice vlan 70
     switchport voice vlan priority 6
     switchport voice dscp 46
awplus#show vlan brief
    70      ckVOICE          STATIC  ACTIVE  port1.0.10(t)
awplus#show lldp interface port1.0.10
     1.0.10   Rx Tx  -- --  10.38.22.34      ---------- -------- -------- McNpLoPe--
awplus#show lldp local-info med interface port1.0.10
      Network Policy ................... Voice
           VLAN ID ..................... 70
awplus#show boot
    Current boot image : flash:/x960-tb277.rel (file exists)
    Current boot config: flash:/default.cfg (file exists)

  After the cold boot, on the wire, which is the verdict.  tools/lldpmed_phone.py was run
  again on eth3 with --no-dhcp and a fresh endpoint MAC ending 53:50, writing
  /tmp/lldp1159/m18_persist.pcap.  A fresh MAC is a new endpoint, so the rebooted switch
  had to answer it from nothing.  What the endpoint heard back:

      1792551657.772 rx LLDPDU from e2:1a:ea:d4:70:43:
          [('port-id', 'port1.0.10'), ('org-e01aea-2', ...), ('org-00120f-1', '038001001e'),
           ('MED-capabilities', '002704'),
           ('MED-network-policy', {'app': 1, 'unknown': 0, 'tagged': 1, 'vlan': 70, 'l2prio': 6, 'dscp': 46})]
      1792551658.773 rx LLDPDU ... (identical)

    persisted                              predicted in the frame        measured
    vlan 70 ckVOICE, tagged on port1.0.10  Network Policy vlan=70,       yes
                                           tagged=1
    switchport voice vlan priority 6       l2prio=6, NOT the default 5   yes
    switchport voice dscp 46               dscp=46                       yes
    LLDP-MED itself                        MED Capabilities 002704,      yes
                                           fast start at 1.00 s

  <-- The priority of 6 is what makes this airtight.  A device that had silently gone
      back to its defaults would advertise 5.
  <-- The boot pointers are unchanged.
  <-- Only swi_d was reloaded.  swi_a and swi_b were not rebooted and were not touched.
  => STEP 8 PASS: the whole LLDP-MED configuration survived a cold boot, and it was
     proven in a frame and not in a show command.  After the reboot the DUT advertised a
     Network Policy carrying vlan 70, tagged, priority 6 and dscp 46, and it still sent
     the fast-start burst at 1.00 s.
